  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mainly dry and seasonable conditions, outside of a \nstray shower this afternoon and Tuesday afternoon.\n\nThe synoptic setup for the next several days is as follows. A mid-\nlevel low, which is easily seen on GOES water vapor imagery across \nnorthern New York, will move into northern New England before \ndrifting east into the Canadian Maritimes by Wednesday. Locally, \nWednesday brings a subtle shortwave ridge. In general, expect benign \nconditions through the first half of Thursday as surface high \npressure expands into the Northeast from the Midwest. However, a \nstray pop-up shower or thunderstorm remains possible this afternoon \nand Tuesday afternoon due to cyclonic flow, which will promote \ndiurnal cu development amid steep low-level lapse rates (greater \nthan 8C/km) along a trough. No severe weather is expected, as mid-\nlevel lapse rates remain poor (less than 6C/km). Areas with the best \nchance of experiencing a brief shower or storm (less than a 25 PoP) \nthrough this afternoon are west of KIJD, KORH, and KFIT, associated \nwith the passage of a weak surface trough. Thereafter, cannot \ndiscredit a shower farther east along the Boston-to-Providence \ncorridor. Similar conditions are expected again Tuesday. Wednesday \ninto Thursday, the mid-level shortwave ridge moves through, \nproviding another dry day Wednesday. However, the ridge shifts east \nby Thursday ahead of an approaching trough, which has the potential \nto bring numerous showers late Thursday into Friday. More on that in \nKEY MESSAGE 2.\n\nAs for temperatures, expect a seasonable week, with afternoon highs \nin the lower 80s and higher-terrain communities in the northern \nWorcester Hills and eastern Berkshires remaining in the middle to \nupper 70s. Nightly lows will be a touch cooler as clear skies and \nlight winds allow for radiational cooling, with temperatures \nslipping back into the 50s while urban areas remain near 60 degrees. \nThis setup may allow for another night of low stratus or fog. \nConfidence in how widespread it becomes remains low; however, it \ndoes not appear this round of fog will be as prevalent as last\nnight.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Main risk for a round of scattered showers and a few \nthunderstorms will be sometime late Thu/Thu night into Fri with \nsummerlike humidity. Mainly dry & seasonable weather favored for the \nweekend.\n\nThe next shortwave trough approaches from the west by late in the \nwork week. Ahead of this approaching shortwave...summerlike humidity \nwill return Thu into Fri. Timing quite uncertain this far out in the \nforecast...But appears there will be the potential for a round of \nscattered showers and a few thunderstorms sometime in the late \nThu/Thu night into Fri time frame. CSU machine learning probs do \nindicate some low probs for severe weather/heavy rainfall \npotential...But that is really just climo for an approaching \nshortwave this time of year. So not much more we can say at this \npoint. Current guidance does indicate that drier/seasonable weather \nis favored to return for the upcoming weekend.",
